      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "diatonic-minor-seventh-ii-iii-vi",
      "narrative": "Carter identifies minor sevenths as the II, III, and VI scale degrees in a major key ('Three minor sevenths II, III and VI'). In cycle-of-fifths practice, minor sevenths should be voiced with independent voice motion -- individual voices moving by half or whole step -- rather than root-position parallel block motion: 'If you think of harmonic movement as several moving voices rather than a glob of dots on a chord box grid, your arrangements and improvisations will be more airy, more interesting and less clunky.' Chapter 9, p. 117; Chapter 13, p. 156.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "alan-de-mause:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book:harmonic-movement-system:voice-leading-over-root-position"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
          "citation": "chapter 9 p.117"
        },
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
          "citation": "chapter 13 p.156"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7",
      "function_role": "ii-chord-in-ii-v7-i",
      "narrative": "Carter prescribes the II-V7-I progression as 'the most common jazz progression unit' and the most idiomatic modulation bridge. The minor seventh on the II degree serves as the harmonic on-ramp before V7: 'putting a II of the upcoming new key in front of the new key V7' is the preferred jazz-language solution for connecting song sections and keys. Chapter 14, p. 166; Chapter 11, p. 141.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "alan-de-mause:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book:harmonic-movement-system:ii-v7-i-modulation",
        "alan-de-mause:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book:self-accompaniment-texture-system:bass-first-conception"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
          "citation": "chapter 14 p.166"
        },
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
          "citation": "chapter 11 p.141"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "b",
      "granularity_name": "voicing-floor"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "min7b5",
      "function_role": "half-diminished-vii-diatonic",
      "narrative": "Carter identifies the half-diminished seventh (min7b5) as the VII chord of the major scale -- 'One half-diminished seventh VII' -- and classifies it among the four diatonic seventh-chord types. In ascending chromatic root-movement contexts, Carter prescribes the diminished seventh (dim7) rather than min7b5 as the connector. Chapter 9, p. 117; Chapter 10, p. 120.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "alan-de-mause:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book:harmonic-movement-system"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
          "citation": "chapter 9 p.117"
        },
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
          "citation": "chapter 10 p.120"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "a",
      "granularity_name": "harmonic-family"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "v7-dominant-resolution",
      "narrative": "Carter prescribes the dominant seventh (V7) as 'the most basic harmonic movement of jazz' and the mandatory gateway chord: 'The last chord of the intro must be a V7 or V7 substitute in order to move into the first chord of the tune itself.' For V7-I resolution, the prescribed voice-leading move is contrary motion: 'As the top note descends by half step the root note has its own opposing pattern, not parallel to the top.' Chapter 13, p. 156; Chapter 14, p. 160.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "alan-de-mause:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book:harmonic-movement-system:contrary-motion-v7-i",
        "alan-de-mause:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book:harmonic-movement-system:tritone-pull-resolution",
        "alan-de-mause:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book:harmonic-movement-system:intro-must-end-on-v7"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
          "citation": "chapter 13 p.156"
        },
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
          "citation": "chapter 14 p.160"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "g",
      "granularity_name": "voice-leading"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "tag-initiation-substitute-for-tonic",
      "narrative": "Carter prescribes V7 as the mandatory substitute for the final I chord when initiating a tag or coda: 'the replacement of the usual final I chord -- E, here -- with a V7 (or other substitute) chord is required.' The trigger is the cue 'Tag!' from the bandleader. Chapter 14, p. 164.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "alan-de-mause:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book:harmonic-movement-system:tag-i-to-v7-substitution"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
      "references": [
        {
          "source": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
          "citation": "chapter 14 p.164"
        }
      ],
      "provenance": "extracted",
      "granularity_level": "e",
      "granularity_name": "substitution-rules"
    },
    {
      "chord_quality": "dom7",
      "function_role": "turnaround-gateway",
      "narrative": "Carter prescribes V7 as the closing chord of every turnaround, treating the turnaround as 'practically another intro': 'Like introductions, turnarounds often start on a I chord and finish on a V7 or V7 substitute chord.' Chapter 14, p. 164.",
      "source_principle_ids": [
        "alan-de-mause:complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book:harmonic-movement-system:intro-must-end-on-v7"
      ],
      "source_work_id": "complete-fingerstyle-jazz-guitar-book",
      "references": [
